--TEST--
`Core\Command::run` converts a `decimal` argument into the exact number the command line wrote, keeping the scale a `float` would lose — ADR 0086 § 6, `rule:types/decimal`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 6's "a matched value's type comes from the parameter", for the type the
// argument matcher had admitted and could not yet convert. The conversion is
// `nvs_runtime::decimal`'s own parse rather than a second one written beside
// the matcher, which is what keeps a word a command line supplies and a
// `{amount: decimal}` segment a route matches (ADR 0102 § 5) on one grammar.
//
// The claim is the *scale*, not merely that a number arrived: `1.10` is two
// decimal places, a `float` argument would print `1.1`, and the doubling is
// what a text argument could not have been asked for at all — a `tainted
// string` does not add.
class Ledger {
    #[Core\Command(name: "deposit", about: "Add to the balance")]
    public static function deposit(decimal $amount): uint {
        decimal $doubled = $amount + $amount;
        echo "amount: ", $amount, "\n";
        echo "doubled: ", $doubled, "\n";
        return 0;
    }
}

// The status is taken first, because the handler writes while `run` is still
// being evaluated.
uint $status = Core\Command::run();
echo "status: ", $status, "\n";
--ARGS--
deposit
1.10
--EXPECT--
amount: 1.10
doubled: 2.20
status: 0
